<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/5th-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Abydos</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">411 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnesian-war">Peloponnesian War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Abydos was a naval battle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnesian-war">Peloponnesian War</a> fought in 411 BCE in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellespont">Hellespont</a> off the city of Abydos. An Athenian fleet under Thrasybulus and Thrasyllus was fighting the Spartan fleet of Mindarus when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alcibiades">Alcibiades</a> arrived with reinforcements and turned the battle into a clear Athenian victory. Troops of the Persian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrap</a> Pharnabazus saved many Spartan ships on the shore.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the Athenian victory at Cynossema, the Athenian fleet set up a base at Sestos, from which it could answer any move by the Spartan fleet at Abydos. The Spartan navarch Mindarus, hoping to end the war with a decisive victory, summoned the Rhodian commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorieus">Dorieus</a> with his 14 ships. Spotted by Athenian lookouts before he reached Abydos, Dorieus was driven ashore: at Rhoeteum according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xenophon">Xenophon</a>, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dardanus">Dardanus</a> according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diodorus-siculus">Diodorus Siculus</a>, while Donald Kagan suggests he was trapped at both in turn.</p><p>Mindarus hurried from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troy">Troy</a>, where he had been sacrificing to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a>, and led his ships out to rescue <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorieus">Dorieus</a>, while the Persian Pharnabazus brought his army up to support Dorieus from the land. Seeing the Spartan ships put out from Abydos, the Athenians set out from Sestos to challenge them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorieus">Dorieus</a>'s ships, Mindarus had 97 ships against 74 Athenian. The Spartans formed up with the Asian shore of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellespont">Hellespont</a> at their backs, Mindarus on the right and the Syracusans on the left; opposite them Thrasybulus led the Athenian right and Thrasyllus the left. Trumpeters relayed the signal to begin, and an evenly matched fight followed, pilots trying to ram and disable enemy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trireme">triremes</a> while marines fought whenever ships came within range.</p><p>Neither side gained the advantage until, as the day wore on, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alcibiades">Alcibiades</a> appeared with 18 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trireme">triremes</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samos">Samos</a>. Both fleets at first thought the ships might be theirs, until Alcibiades ran up a red flag, the agreed signal to the Athenians. The Spartan fleet fled towards Abydos and suffered heavy losses as the Athenians attacked its strung-out ships. In all the Athenians captured 30 Spartan ships and recovered the 15 of their own lost at Cynossema.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Mindarus returned to Abydos to repair his fleet and sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> for reinforcements, planning new campaigns with Pharnabazus. The Athenians could not press their advantage: with their treasury low and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euboea">Euboea</a> in revolt, they sent 30 ships under Theramenes, who failed to stop the rebels building a causeway to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a> but raised much money by plunder. Soon afterwards the satrap Tissaphernes arrested <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alcibiades">Alcibiades</a>, who had sailed to meet him with gifts, and imprisoned him at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardis">Sardis</a>; he escaped within a month, but his claims of influence with the satrap were destroyed.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Abydos” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It joined the Delian League but revolted in 411 BCE, becoming a Spartan base, and the <b>Battle of Abydos</b> was fought off its shore that year.</blockquote><a href="/state/abydos">Abydos</a></li></ul></section>
